  "evaluation": "A is strongest: it recognizes that Study A reports improvement rather than harm and identifies the relevant outcome-measure distinction between a depression dimension within a quality-of-life instrument and questionnaire depression scores. C reaches the same defensible verdict and reason, but emphasizes score-direction miscoding rather than clearly grounding apparent disagreement in the permitted different-constructs case. B correctly identifies design and population differences, but overlooks both Study A's explicitly improved score and the outcome-measure distinction, making its genuine-disagreement verdict less convincing. Both findings concern vitamin D; the combination supplement does not make Study A off-question.",
  "ranking": ["A", "C", "B"]
}
